module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.DirectSum.Decomposition
public import Mathlib.Algebra.GradedMulAction
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Constructions.ProjModuleRestriction
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Veronese
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# The Veronese subring and submodule are graded by the reindexed components

Let `S = ⨁_{i ≥ 0} S_i` be a graded ring and let `d ≥ 1`. The Veronese subring
`S⁽ᵈ⁾ ⊆ S` is the subring generated by the homogeneous elements whose degree is a
multiple of `d` (`Stacks.Veronese.veroneseSubring`). This file proves that `S⁽ᵈ⁾` is
again a graded ring, with degree-`n` component the *original* degree-`nd`
component `S_{nd}`, and the analogous statement for the Veronese part
`T⁽ᵈ⁾ = ⨁_n T_{nd}` of a `ℤ`-graded `S`-module `T`.

These two gradings are what makes `Proj S⁽ᵈ⁾` and the comparison of degree-zero
localizations available at all: without them there is no `GradedRing` instance on
`S⁽ᵈ⁾`, hence no `HomogeneousLocalization` and no `ProjectiveSpectrum` for it.

With the gradings in hand the file then proves that passing to a positive Veronese grading
does not change the degree-zero localization at a positive-degree homogeneous element. For
`f ∈ S_e` with `e > 0` the element `f ^ d` is homogeneous of Veronese degree `e`, and the
inclusion `S⁽ᵈ⁾ ⊆ S` induces a ring isomorphism

  `(S⁽ᵈ⁾)₍f ᵈ₎ ≃+* S₍f₎`,  `x / (f ᵈ)ⁿ ↦ x / f ⁿᵈ`,

together with a compatible additive isomorphism `(T⁽ᵈ⁾)₍f ᵈ₎ ≃+ T₍f₎` of the degree-zero
localizations of a graded module, semilinear over the ring isomorphism. Both isomorphisms are
natural for the restriction maps of Tag 01M4 attached to an inclusion `D₊(g) ⊆ D₊(f)`, which
on the Veronese side is the inclusion `D₊(g ᵈ) ⊆ D₊(f ᵈ)`. This is the algebraic input to the
proof that `Proj S⁽ᵈ⁾ ≅ Proj S` for `S` generated in degree one after a Veronese twist.

## Main definitions

* `DirectSum.decompositionProj` is the `i`-th homogeneous projection attached to
  any `DirectSum.Decomposition`, as an additive map. It generalizes Mathlib's
  `GradedRing.proj`, which needs the full `GradedRing` structure.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseSubmonoid` realizes the generating set
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseSet` as a submonoid of `S`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseGrading` is the degree-`n` component of `S⁽ᵈ⁾`, namely
  the elements of `S⁽ᵈ⁾` lying in `S_{nd}`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseSubmodule` is the Veronese part `T⁽ᵈ⁾` of a `ℤ`-graded
  module, as a `S⁽ᵈ⁾`-submodule.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleGrading` is the degree-`n` component of `T⁽ᵈ⁾`,
  namely the elements of `T⁽ᵈ⁾` lying in `T_{nd}`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.instVAddNatInt` is the action of a non-negative ring degree on an
  integer module degree, by addition. Mathlib has no `VAdd ℕ ℤ` instance.
* `Stacks.Veronese.awayModule` is the action of `S₍f₎` on `T_f`, obtained from the
  embedding `S₍f₎ ↪ S_f`. It is a `local instance`: Mathlib does not make
  `Module.compHom` an instance, and this file needs the action available to
  typeclass inference in order to speak about `GradedModuleDegreeZeroLocalization`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veronesePower` is `f ^ d` viewed as an element of `S⁽ᵈ⁾`; it is
  homogeneous of Veronese degree `deg f`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseLocalizationMap` is the induced map `S⁽ᵈ⁾_{f ᵈ} → S_f` of
  ordinary localizations, and `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseLocalizedModuleFun` the induced
  map `T⁽ᵈ⁾_{f ᵈ} → T_f` of localized modules.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseNumDen`, `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseAwayFun` and
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseAwayHom` build the comparison of degree-zero localizations
  on homogeneous fractions.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseAwayEquiv` is the ring isomorphism
  `(S⁽ᵈ⁾)₍f ᵈ₎ ≃+* S₍f₎`, and `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleAwayEquiv` the additive
  isomorphism `(T⁽ᵈ⁾)₍f ᵈ₎ ≃+ T₍f₎`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Veronese.mem_veroneseSubring_iff` identifies `S⁽ᵈ⁾` with the additive
  subgroup generated by the `S_{nd}`: the subring closure adds nothing, because
  the generating set is already multiplicatively closed.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseGradingGradedRing` is the `GradedRing` structure on
  `S⁽ᵈ⁾` with components `S_{nd}`, obtained from
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseGrading_isInternal`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleGradingDecomposition` is the corresponding
  `DirectSum.Decomposition` of `T⁽ᵈ⁾`, obtained from
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleGrading_isInternal`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleGrading_gradedSMul` says the two reindexed
  gradings are compatible with the scalar action.
* `Stacks.Veronese.basicOpen_veronesePower_le` transports an inclusion of standard
  opens `D₊(g) ⊆ D₊(f)` to the Veronese projective spectrum.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseAwayEquiv_mk` and
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleAwayEquiv_mk` identify the two comparisons on
  fractions: `x / (f ᵈ)ⁿ ↦ x / f ⁿᵈ`.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleAwayEquiv_smul` is the semilinearity of the module
  comparison over the ring comparison.
* `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseAwayEquiv_naturality` and
  `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseModuleAwayEquiv_naturality` are compatibility with the
  restriction maps `ProjectiveSpectrum.degreeZeroRingMap` and
  `ProjectiveSpectrum.moduleRestrictionMaps` of Tag 01M4.
* `Stacks.Veronese.mem_gradedModuleDegreeZeroLocalization_iff` describes `T₍f₎` as the
  set of fractions `x / f ⁿ` with `x` homogeneous of degree `n · deg f`; it is used
  for both gradings.

## Implementation notes

`d > 0` is used exactly once in each internality proof, and only for injectivity:
it is what makes `n ↦ n * d` injective, so that distinct Veronese degrees are
distinct degrees of `S`. Surjectivity holds for every `d`, including `d = 0`.

The gradings are constructed as genuine `GradedRing` and
`DirectSum.Decomposition` *data*, not merely as `Nonempty` existence statements.
This is deliberate: a `Nonempty` wrapped in `Classical.choice` would make every
downstream statement mentioning the grading depend on the existence proof, so a
single unproved existence lemma poisons the statement of everything built on it.
Since `DirectSum.Decomposition` is a `Subsingleton`, the choice is harmless but
the indirection is not.

Universes are not narrowed anywhere: the ring, the module and the `SetLike`
carrier type each keep their own universe, exactly as in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Veronese`.

`veroneseSubmodule` is stated for `ℳ : ℤ → AddSubgroup M` with
`[DirectSum.Decomposition ℳ]` and `[SetLike.GradedSMul 𝒜 ℳ]`, matching the
`ℕ`-graded-ring-on-`ℤ`-graded-module convention used throughout this
development; the `SetLike.GradedSMul` hypothesis is stated against
`Stacks.Veronese.instVAddNatInt`.

The comparison statements take the grading of `S⁽ᵈ⁾` as the *typeclass hypothesis*
`[GradedRing (veroneseGrading 𝒜 d)]` (and, on the module side,
`[DirectSum.Decomposition (veroneseModuleGrading 𝒜 ℳ d)]`) rather than inlining
`veroneseGradingGradedRing 𝒜 d hd`. This is strictly more general — the canonical
instances above are what one plugs in — and it is what makes the statements usable
without rewriting along a `letI`. Where `0 < d` is still needed it appears as an
explicit hypothesis `hd`, namely exactly where the inequality `n ≤ nd` is used to
replace a power of `f` by a power of `f ^ d`.

`veroneseLocalizationMap` is `IsLocalization.map` along the inclusion
`S⁽ᵈ⁾ ↪ S`, which is legitimate because `(f ^ d) ^ n = f ^ (dn)` sends powers of
`f ^ d` into powers of `f`. The comparison of *degree-zero* localizations is then
built on `HomogeneousLocalization.NumDenSameDeg` by `Quotient.map'`, reindexing the
degree `n ↦ nd` and keeping the numerator and denominator: the degree is invisible
to the quotient relation, so `veroneseLocalizationMap` witnesses well-definedness,
injectivity and surjectivity all at once. Mathlib's `HomogeneousLocalization.map`
cannot be used, since it requires a graded ring hom with the *same* index monoid
and the Veronese comparison reindexes degrees.

`mem_gradedModuleDegreeZeroLocalization_iff` duplicates the private lemma
`ProjectiveSpectrum.mem_degreeZero_iff` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Constructions.ProjModuleRestriction`, which is not
accessible outside that file; the two should be merged when that file is next
touched.

